// The Venus factions as diplomacy SIDES, authored declaratively.
//
// Declared from //shared/signal/create_sides in __init__.mast, the same route LM uses, so
// they exist before player ships and before any map runs.
//
// A side is NOT the same thing as the faction name on a hull. shipData `side` (and
// `origin`, and therefore `race`) says what a ship IS - it is how a prefab finds "an Armada
// raider" and how taunts are looked up. THIS file says who shoots whom. They share names
// here because that reads better, not because the systems are the same.
//
// Relations are declared from BOTH ends deliberately. The engine's table is DIRECTIONAL
// (coloring looks up viewer-side -> contact-side), and although side_set_relations mirrors
// each pair for you, LM's own sides.amd carries the same duplication and its comment warns
// not to "tidy it up" as redundant. Do not.
//
// THE GUILD IS THE INTERESTING ONE. It is hostile to nobody, which is the point: the free
// cities sell lifting gas to whoever moors, and a mission's drama comes from what the two
// fleets are willing to do about that. A story that wants the Guild to pick a side should
// flip it at runtime with side_set_relations rather than editing this file, so the choice
// belongs to the mission.

# [Kingdom](kingdom)
---
Color: #DA4
Enemies: armada
---
The Sky Kingdom. Brass, gas and protocol: a chartered royal navy of dirigibles that holds
the high trade lanes and believes, sincerely, that it is keeping the peace.

# [Armada](armada)
---
Color: #C22
Enemies: kingdom
---
The Pirate Armada. Salvage plating over plasma engines, flying a black flag out of the deep
deck. Not an empire - a market with guns, and every hull in it was somebody else's first.

# [Guild](guild)
---
Color: #3BA
---
The Free Cities. Neutral by treaty and by necessity. They sell lifting gas, water ice and
harbor to anyone who moors peacefully, and they have outlived three kingdoms doing it.
